CS=lambda lab,cap,extra='': S(lab,'',None,cap,NIGHT2,COSMIC(110,96,.95)+extra)
VA=[
X(1,'MY DELUSION IS GONE',
 S('Arjuna with folded hands, a dark cloud lifting from his mind','Af','light','MOHA GONE'),
 'Arjuna said: Out of grace to me you have spoken the supreme secret called adhyātma; by those words of yours this delusion of mine is gone.',
 [('mat-anugrahāya','<b>out of grace to me</b>'),('paramam guhyam','<b>the supreme secret</b>'),('adhyātma-saṃjñitam','<b>called adhyātma</b>'),('yat tvayā uktam vacaḥ','<b>the words you spoke</b>'),('mohaḥ ayam vigataḥ mama','<b>this delusion of mine is gone</b>')],
 'After hearing chapters 7–10, Arjuna feels his confusion lifting. He opens chapter 11 by thanking Kṛṣṇa.',
 'Arjuna says “out of grace to me”: he now sees the teaching as a gift, not an argument he must win. Commentators note his delusion about death (chapter 2) and about God (chapters 7–10) has eased. Yet one thing remains: he has heard of the Lord’s glory, but not seen it. That longing drives this chapter.',
 'Gratitude is the right response to good teaching. Before asking for more, Arjuna thanks his teacher. It is a good habit for any student.',
 'Arjuna: <b>my delusion is gone</b>.'),
X(2,'YOUR GREATNESS',
 S('Arjuna remembering what he heard: worlds rising and dissolving in Krishna','A','wheel','I HAVE HEARD'),
 '“I have heard from you in detail of the origin and dissolution of beings, lotus-eyed one, and also of your imperishable greatness.”',
 [('bhava-apyayau hi bhūtānām','<b>the origin and end of beings</b>'),('śrutau vistaraśaḥ mayā','<b>have been heard in detail by me</b>'),('tvattaḥ kamala-patra-akṣa','<b>from you, lotus-eyed one</b>'),('māhātmyam api ca avyayam','<b>and your imperishable greatness</b>')],
 'Arjuna summarises what he has learned.',
 'Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa <i>kamala-patra-akṣa</i>, “lotus-petal-eyed”, a loving epithet. He has heard; now he wants to see. Commentators note the classic sequence of learning: hearing (<i>śravaṇa</i>), then reflecting and seeing.',
 'Hearing about something is the first step. A longing to see for oneself is a good sign.',
 'I have heard <b>your greatness</b>.'),
X(3,'I WISH TO SEE IT',
 S('Arjuna looking up longingly at a glowing outline of a cosmic form','A','cosmic','I WISH TO SEE'),
 '“It is as you have described yourself, supreme Lord. Yet I wish to see your divine form, O Supreme Person.”',
 [('evam etat yathā āttha tvam','<b>it is just as you say</b>'),('ātmānam parama-īśvara','<b>of yourself, supreme Lord</b>'),('draṣṭum icchāmi te rūpam','<b>I wish to see your form</b>'),('aiśvaram puruṣottama','<b>divine, O Supreme Person</b>')],
 'Arjuna makes his request: show me your divine form.',
 'He first affirms faith (“it is as you say”), then asks to see. Commentators note the order: not doubt seeking proof, but faith seeking sight. The word <i>aiśvara</i> means sovereign, lordly: Arjuna wants to see God as Lord of all.',
 'It is natural to want to see what we believe. Faith and the wish for experience can go together.',
 'Faith asks: <b>let me see</b>.'),
X(4,'IF YOU THINK I CAN',
 S('Arjuna bowing humbly, asking only if he is able','Af','heart','IF I CAN'),
 '“If you think it possible for me to see it, Lord, master of yoga, then show me your imperishable Self.”',
 [('manyase yadi tat śakyam','<b>if you think it possible</b>'),('mayā draṣṭum iti prabho','<b>for me to see, Lord</b>'),('yoga-īśvara tataḥ me tvam','<b>master of yoga, then you to me</b>'),('darśaya ātmānam avyayam','<b>show your imperishable Self</b>')],
 'Arjuna asks humbly, leaving the decision to Kṛṣṇa.',
 'Commentators admire the humility: “if you think I am fit”. Arjuna does not demand. He calls Kṛṣṇa <i>yogeśvara</i>, master of yoga, a title used again at the very end (18.78).',
 'Ask humbly and leave the timing to the wise. Not every gift is ready when we want it.',
 '“If you think <b>I am able</b>.”'),
X(5,'BEHOLD MY FORMS',
 CS('Hundreds of forms of many colours appearing in a vast cosmic figure','SEE!'),
 'The Lord said: Behold, Pārtha, my forms by the hundreds and thousands, divine, of many kinds, of many colours and shapes.',
 [('paśya me pārtha rūpāṇi','<b>behold my forms</b>'),('śataśaḥ atha sahasraśaḥ','<b>by hundreds and thousands</b>'),('nānā-vidhāni divyāni','<b>of many kinds, divine</b>'),('nānā-varṇa-ākṛtīni ca','<b>of many colours and shapes</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa agrees, and begins to reveal.',
 'Four times in four verses Kṛṣṇa says <i>paśya</i>, “behold!”. Commentators feel the excitement: the teacher is eager to show. The forms are countless and varied: the One appears as the many.',
 'Diversity in the world is not chaos; it is the many forms of one reality.',
 '<b>Behold</b> my thousand forms.'),
X(6,'WONDERS NEVER SEEN',
 CS('Gods of many groups visible within the cosmic form','NEVER SEEN BEFORE'),
 '“Behold the Ādityas, the Vasus, the Rudras, the two Aśvins and the Maruts; behold many wonders never seen before, Bhārata.”',
 [('paśya ādityān vasūn rudrān','<b>behold Ādityas, Vasus, Rudras</b>'),('aśvinau marutaḥ tathā','<b>the Aśvins and the Maruts</b>'),('bahūni adṛṣṭa-pūrvāṇi','<b>many never seen before</b>'),('paśya āścaryāṇi bhārata','<b>behold wonders</b>')],
 'All the Vedic gods are within him.',
 'The Ādityas (12), Vasus (8), Rudras (11) and the Aśvins (2) make the traditional 33 Vedic gods; the Maruts are storm gods. Commentators: all the gods are aspects of one Lord, as chapter 10 said.',
 'Every power in nature is part of one whole.',
 'All the gods <b>within one form</b>.'),
X(7,'THE WHOLE UNIVERSE IN ONE PLACE',
 CS('The entire universe, stars and worlds, inside one cosmic body','ALL IN ONE'),
 '“See here today, Guḍākeśa, the whole universe, moving and unmoving, gathered in one place in my body, and whatever else you wish to see.”',
 [('iha ekastham jagat kṛtsnam','<b>here, the whole universe in one place</b>'),('paśya adya sa-cara-acaram','<b>behold today, moving and unmoving</b>'),('mama dehe guḍākeśa','<b>in my body</b>'),('yat ca anyat draṣṭum icchasi','<b>and whatever else you wish to see</b>')],
 'Everything in one place: the whole cosmos in God’s body.',
 '“Whatever else you wish to see”: commentators say this includes the outcome of the war, which Arjuna will indeed see (11.26–28). Rāmānuja’s idea of the world as God’s body is vividly pictured here.',
 'Imagine all that exists held together as one body. That is the Gītā’s vision of unity.',
 'The universe <b>in one body</b>.'),
X(8,'THE DIVINE EYE',
 S('Krishna touching Arjuna’s forehead, a divine eye opening','KA','light','DIVYAM CAKṢUḤ'),
 '“But you cannot see me with these eyes of yours. I give you a divine eye: behold my sovereign yoga!”',
 [('na tu mām śakyase draṣṭum','<b>but you cannot see me</b>'),('anena eva sva-cakṣuṣā','<b>with these your own eyes</b>'),('divyam dadāmi te cakṣuḥ','<b>I give you a divine eye</b>'),('paśya me yogam aiśvaram','<b>behold my sovereign yoga</b>')],
 'Ordinary eyes cannot see the divine form. Kṛṣṇa gives Arjuna divine sight.',
 'Note the parallel: Sañjaya too received divine sight from Vyāsa to see the battle. Commentators say the divine eye is not physical but a gift of grace. Some read it as the eye of knowledge; others as a real, special vision.',
 'Some things can only be seen with new eyes. Ask for the vision to see what matters.',
 'I give you <b>a divine eye</b>.'),
X(9,'HARI SHOWS HIS FORM',
 S('Sanjaya reporting to the blind king as Krishna transforms','S','cosmic','HARI'),
 'Sañjaya said: Having spoken thus, O king, Hari, the great Lord of yoga, showed Pārtha his supreme, sovereign form.',
 [('evam uktvā tataḥ rājan','<b>having spoken thus, then, O king</b>'),('mahā-yoga-īśvaraḥ hariḥ','<b>Hari, the great Lord of yoga</b>'),('darśayām āsa pārthāya','<b>showed to Pārtha</b>'),('paramam rūpam aiśvaram','<b>the supreme sovereign form</b>')],
 'Sañjaya interrupts to tell the blind king what happened.',
 'A powerful irony: Sañjaya describes the cosmic form to Dhṛtarāṣṭra, who is blind both physically and spiritually. Commentators note Sañjaya himself saw it through Vyāsa’s gift. <i>Hari</i>, “the one who takes away” sorrow and sin.',
 'Even when we cannot see, someone may describe the light to us. Listen.',
 'Hari shows <b>his supreme form</b>.'),
X(10,'MANY MOUTHS AND EYES',
 CS('A vast form with many faces and eyes, ornaments and weapons','ANEKA'),
 '“With many mouths and eyes, many wondrous sights, many divine ornaments, many divine weapons raised,”',
 [('aneka-vaktra-nayanam','<b>with many mouths and eyes</b>'),('aneka-adbhuta-darśanam','<b>with many wondrous sights</b>'),('aneka-divya-ābharaṇam','<b>with many divine ornaments</b>'),('divya-aneka-udyata-āyudham','<b>with many divine weapons raised</b>')],
 'Sañjaya begins describing the form, piling up “many”.',
 'The word <i>aneka</i>, “many”, repeats like a drumbeat. Commentators say language strains to describe the infinite. The sentence continues into the next verse.',
 'Some experiences are too big for words. The Gītā shows us words straining.',
 'Many mouths, <b>many eyes</b>.'),
X(11,'FACING EVERYWHERE',
 CS('The cosmic form wearing garlands, anointed with fragrance, facing every direction','VIŚVATO-MUKHA'),
 '“…wearing divine garlands and garments, anointed with divine fragrances, all-wondrous, resplendent, infinite, facing every direction.”',
 [('divya-mālya-ambara-dharam','<b>wearing divine garlands and robes</b>'),('divya-gandha-anulepanam','<b>anointed with divine fragrance</b>'),('sarva-āścarya-mayam','<b>full of all wonders</b>'),('devam anantam','<b>the infinite God</b>'),('viśvato-mukham','<b>facing every direction</b>')],
 'Beauty as well as power: garlands, robes and fragrance.',
 'The form is not only awesome but adorned like a deity in a temple. Commentators note it engages every sense: sight, smell, wonder. <i>Viśvato-mukha</i>, facing everywhere, means no place is outside his gaze.',
 'The Divine is both majestic and beautiful. Both awe and love are right responses.',
 'Infinite, <b>facing everywhere</b>.'),
X(12,'A THOUSAND SUNS',
 S('A thousand suns blazing at once in the sky','','sun','1000 SUNS',NIGHT2,''.join(SUN(30+k*40,30+(k%2)*20,10) for k in range(5))),
 '“If the light of a thousand suns were to blaze forth at once in the sky, it might resemble the splendour of that great Being.”',
 [('divi sūrya-sahasrasya','<b>in the sky, of a thousand suns</b>'),('bhavet yugapat utthitā','<b>should rise at once</b>'),('yadi bhāḥ sadṛśī sā syāt','<b>if the light, it might resemble</b>'),('bhāsaḥ tasya mahātmanaḥ','<b>the splendour of that great Being</b>')],
 'One of the most famous images in the Gītā.',
 'J. Robert Oppenheimer recalled this verse (with 11.32) after the first atomic test in 1945, which made it known worldwide. In the Gītā the image speaks of divine glory beyond all earthly light. Commentators note even “a thousand suns” is only a hint: “it might resemble”.',
 'Some realities outshine all comparison. Let wonder remain wonder.',
 'Brighter than <b>a thousand suns</b>.'),
X(13,'ALL IN ONE',
 CS('Arjuna seeing the whole universe divided into countless parts within one body','EKASTHAM'),
 '“There Pāṇḍava saw the whole universe, with its many divisions, gathered in one place in the body of the God of gods.”',
 [('tatra ekastham jagat kṛtsnam','<b>there, the whole universe in one place</b>'),('pravibhaktam anekadhā','<b>divided in many ways</b>'),('apaśyat deva-devasya','<b>he saw, of the God of gods</b>'),('śarīre pāṇḍavaḥ tadā','<b>in the body, Pāṇḍava, then</b>')],
 'Arjuna sees unity in diversity.',
 'The universe keeps all its divisions, yet rests in one body. Commentators see the heart of Vedānta here: the many are real as forms, yet one in their ground.',
 'Unity does not erase differences. It holds them.',
 'Many, <b>held in one</b>.'),
X(14,'HAIR ON END',
 S('Arjuna bowing with folded hands, overwhelmed with wonder','Af','cosmic','VISMAYA'),
 '“Then Dhanañjaya, filled with wonder, his hair standing on end, bowed his head to the Lord and, with folded hands, spoke.”',
 [('tataḥ saḥ vismaya-āviṣṭaḥ','<b>then he, filled with wonder</b>'),('hṛṣṭa-romā dhanañjayaḥ','<b>hair on end, Arjuna</b>'),('praṇamya śirasā devam','<b>bowing his head to the Lord</b>'),('kṛta-añjaliḥ abhāṣata','<b>with folded hands, spoke</b>')],
 'Arjuna’s response: wonder, goose-flesh, bowing.',
 'Compare 1.29, where Arjuna’s hair stood on end from grief. Now it does from wonder. Commentators see the arc of the Gītā: the same body, transformed by a new vision.',
 'Wonder humbles us. Bowing is the body’s natural answer.',
 'Hair on end, <b>from wonder</b>.'),
X(15,'I SEE ALL THE GODS',
 CS('Arjuna describing gods, Brahma on a lotus, sages and serpents within the form','I SEE!',MOUNT('lotus',110,150,.8)),
 'Arjuna said: I see in your body, O God, all the gods and the hosts of beings; Brahmā the Lord seated on the lotus, all the sages and the divine serpents.',
 [('paśyāmi devān tava deva dehe','<b>I see the gods in your body, O God</b>'),('sarvān tathā bhūta-viśeṣa-saṅghān','<b>and all the hosts of beings</b>'),('brahmāṇam īśam kamala-āsana-stham','<b>Brahmā the Lord on the lotus seat</b>'),('ṛṣīn ca sarvān uragān ca divyān','<b>all the sages and divine serpents</b>')],
 'Arjuna now describes what he sees, in the longer triṣṭubh metre.',
 'The metre changes to triṣṭubh for much of this chapter, giving it a grand, hymn-like feel. Brahmā the creator sits on a lotus, which in Purāṇic images grows from Viṣṇu’s navel. Commentators note even the creator is inside this form.',
 'The creator too is part of a greater whole.',
 'Brahmā <b>on the lotus, within you</b>.'),
X(16,'NO BEGINNING, MIDDLE OR END',
 CS('Countless arms, bellies, mouths and eyes, with no edge','NO END!'),
 '“I see you, with countless arms, bellies, mouths and eyes, of endless form on every side. I see no end, no middle and no beginning of you, Lord of the universe, Form of the universe.”',
 [('aneka-bāhu-udara-vaktra-netram','<b>with many arms, bellies, mouths, eyes</b>'),('paśyāmi tvām sarvataḥ ananta-rūpam','<b>I see you, endless in form on all sides</b>'),('na antam na madhyam na punaḥ tava ādim','<b>no end, no middle, nor beginning</b>'),('viśva-īśvara viśva-rūpa','<b>Lord of all, Form of all</b>')],
 'The form has no limits.',
 '<i>Viśvarūpa</i>, the universal form, names the chapter. Commentators: the eye cannot find an edge because there is none.',
 'Some things cannot be measured. Infinity is one of them.',
 'No beginning, <b>no end</b>.'),
X(17,'A MASS OF RADIANCE',
 CS('A crowned form with mace and discus, blazing too bright to look at','HARD TO SEE'),
 '“I see you with crown, mace and discus, a mass of radiance glowing everywhere, hard to behold, blazing on all sides like flaming fire and sun, immeasurable.”',
 [('kirīṭinam gadinam cakriṇam ca','<b>crowned, with mace and discus</b>'),('tejaḥ-rāśim sarvataḥ dīptimantam','<b>a mass of light blazing everywhere</b>'),('paśyāmi tvām durnirīkṣyam','<b>I see you, hard to look at</b>'),('dīpta-anala-arka-dyutim aprameyam','<b>like blazing fire and sun, immeasurable</b>')],
 'Amid the vastness, Arjuna glimpses Viṣṇu’s familiar emblems: crown, mace, discus.',
 'These details matter later: in 11.46 Arjuna will ask to see this four-armed form again. Commentators note how the infinite and the familiar mingle.',
 'Even in the overwhelming, look for the familiar face of the Divine.',
 'Crown, mace, discus, <b>blazing light</b>.'),
X(18,'GUARDIAN OF ETERNAL DHARMA',
 CS('Arjuna praising the form as the imperishable guardian of dharma','ŚĀŚVATA-DHARMA-GOPTĀ'),
 '“You are the Imperishable, the supreme to be known; you are the final resting-place of this universe. You are the undying guardian of eternal dharma; you are, I believe, the eternal Person.”',
 [('tvam akṣaram paramam veditavyam','<b>you are the Imperishable, the Supreme to be known</b>'),('tvam asya viśvasya param nidhānam','<b>the supreme resting-place of this universe</b>'),('tvam avyayaḥ śāśvata-dharma-goptā','<b>the undying guardian of eternal dharma</b>'),('sanātanaḥ tvam puruṣaḥ mataḥ me','<b>you are the eternal Person, I believe</b>')],
 'Arjuna names what he sees: the guardian of dharma.',
 'This links back to 4.8: the Lord comes to protect dharma. Now Arjuna sees him as its eternal guardian. Commentators note the shift from fear to understanding.',
 'Behind the world’s order stands a guardian. That can give courage.',
 'Guardian of <b>eternal dharma</b>.'),
X(19,'SUN AND MOON ARE YOUR EYES',
 CS('A cosmic face with sun and moon as eyes, fire as mouth','SUN · MOON',SUN(80,40,8)+MOON(140,40,7)),
 '“I see you without beginning, middle or end, of infinite power, with countless arms, the sun and moon your eyes, blazing fire your mouth, heating this universe with your radiance.”',
 [('anādi-madhya-antam ananta-vīryam','<b>without beginning, middle, end, of infinite power</b>'),('ananta-bāhum śaśi-sūrya-netram','<b>endless arms, moon and sun your eyes</b>'),('dīpta-hutāśa-vaktram','<b>blazing fire your mouth</b>'),('sva-tejasā viśvam idam tapantam','<b>heating this world with your light</b>')],
 'Sun and moon are his eyes; fire is his mouth.',
 'This image echoes the Puruṣa Sūkta, where the moon is born of the Puruṣa’s mind and the sun from his eye (see our Puruṣa Sūktam article). Commentators: the cosmos is God’s body.',
 'Look at the sun and moon tonight and remember this verse.',
 'Sun and moon <b>are your eyes</b>.'),
X(20,'THE THREE WORLDS TREMBLE',
 CS('Earth, sky and heaven trembling, filled by the form','THEY TREMBLE'),
 '“The space between heaven and earth and all the directions are filled by you alone. Seeing this wondrous, terrible form of yours, great soul, the three worlds tremble.”',
 [('dyāvā-pṛthivyoḥ idam antaram hi','<b>this space between heaven and earth</b>'),('vyāptam tvayā ekena diśaḥ ca sarvāḥ','<b>is filled by you alone, and all directions</b>'),('dṛṣṭvā adbhutam rūpam ugram tava idam','<b>seeing this wondrous, fierce form of yours</b>'),('loka-trayam pravyathitam','<b>the three worlds tremble</b>')],
 'Wonder turns toward fear.',
 'The words <i>adbhuta</i> (wondrous) and <i>ugra</i> (fierce) stand together. Commentators note the Divine includes both beauty and terror; the Gītā does not hide the fearsome side.',
 'Reality is not only gentle. Facing its power honestly is part of maturity.',
 'Wondrous and <b>terrible</b>.'),
X(21,'THE GODS ENTER YOU',
 CS('Hosts of gods entering the form, sages chanting svasti with folded hands','SVASTI!'),
 '“These hosts of gods enter you; some, afraid, praise you with folded hands. Bands of great sages and perfected ones cry ‘Svasti!’ (may it be well) and praise you with abundant hymns.”',
 [('amī hi tvām sura-saṅghāḥ viśanti','<b>these hosts of gods enter you</b>'),('kecit bhītāḥ prāñjalayaḥ gṛṇanti','<b>some, afraid, praise with folded hands</b>'),('svasti iti uktvā','<b>saying “may it be well”</b>'),('stuvanti tvām stutibhiḥ puṣkalābhiḥ','<b>praise you with many hymns</b>')],
 'Even the gods are drawn into him; sages bless and praise.',
 '<i>Svasti</i> is a blessing, “may all be well”, the root of the svastika symbol. Commentators: the wise respond to divine power with praise and goodwill, not panic.',
 'In awe, bless. “May it be well” is a good prayer in any storm.',
 '<b>“Svasti!”</b>: may all be well.'),
X(22,'ALL BEINGS GAZE IN AMAZEMENT',
 CS('Every class of being looking up in astonishment','VISMITĀḤ'),
 '“The Rudras, Ādityas, Vasus, Sādhyas, Viśvedevas, the two Aśvins, the Maruts, the ancestors, and hosts of gandharvas, yakṣas, asuras and siddhas all gaze at you in amazement.”',
 [('rudra-ādityāḥ vasavaḥ ye ca sādhyāḥ','<b>Rudras, Ādityas, Vasus, Sādhyas</b>'),('viśve aśvinau marutaḥ ca ūṣmapāḥ ca','<b>Viśvedevas, Aśvins, Maruts, ancestors</b>'),('gandharva-yakṣa-asura-siddha-saṅghāḥ','<b>gandharvas, yakṣas, asuras, siddhas</b>'),('vīkṣante tvām vismitāḥ ca eva sarve','<b>all gaze at you in amazement</b>')],
 'Every class of being, divine and demonic, stares in wonder.',
 '<i>Ūṣmapāḥ</i>, “drinkers of warm food”, is a name for the ancestors. Commentators note asuras are included: even the enemies of the gods are awed.',
 'Before the infinite, all differences fall away into wonder.',
 'All beings <b>stand amazed</b>.'),
X(23,'TERRIBLE TEETH',
 CS('Many mouths with fearsome teeth, the worlds and Arjuna trembling','I TREMBLE'),
 '“Seeing your great form, mighty-armed one, with many mouths and eyes, many arms, thighs and feet, many bellies and many terrible teeth, the worlds tremble, and so do I.”',
 [('rūpam mahat te bahu-vaktra-netram','<b>your great form, many mouths and eyes</b>'),('bahu-bāhu-ūru-pādam','<b>many arms, thighs, feet</b>'),('bahu-udaram bahu-daṃṣṭrā-karālam','<b>many bellies, fearsome with many teeth</b>'),('lokāḥ pravyathitāḥ tathā aham','<b>the worlds tremble, and so do I</b>')],
 'Arjuna admits his own fear.',
 'The vision now shows its fierce side: teeth that consume. Commentators see time and death, the destroyers of all, in this image. Arjuna is honest: “and so do I”.',
 'It is honest to admit fear. Arjuna, the bravest, does.',
 '<b>I tremble too</b>.'),
X(24,'NO PEACE, NO COURAGE',
 CS('A blazing many-coloured form touching the sky, Arjuna shaking','NO PEACE'),
 '“Seeing you touching the sky, blazing in many colours, mouths wide open, with huge flaming eyes, my inmost self trembles, Viṣṇu, and I find neither courage nor peace.”',
 [('nabhaḥ-spṛśam dīptam aneka-varṇam','<b>touching the sky, blazing, many-coloured</b>'),('vyātta-ānanam dīpta-viśāla-netram','<b>mouths wide open, eyes huge and blazing</b>'),('pravyathita-antara-ātmā','<b>my inner self shaken</b>'),('dhṛtim na vindāmi śamam ca viṣṇo','<b>I find no courage nor peace, Viṣṇu</b>')],
 'Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa “Viṣṇu” in his terror.',
 'This is the first time Arjuna names Kṛṣṇa as Viṣṇu in the Gītā. Commentators: in extremity, he recognises who stands before him. Courage and peace both desert him.',
 'In fear we often find out who we really trust.',
 'No courage, <b>no peace</b>.'),
X(25,'BE GRACIOUS',
 CS('Fiery mouths like the fire of world-ending, Arjuna pleading','PRASĪDA!'),
 '“Seeing your mouths with terrible teeth, like the fire at the end of time, I lose my sense of direction and find no shelter. Be gracious, Lord of gods, refuge of the world.”',
 [('daṃṣṭrā-karālāni ca te mukhāni','<b>your mouths fearsome with teeth</b>'),('kāla-anala-sannibhāni','<b>like the fire of time’s end</b>'),('diśaḥ na jāne na labhe ca śarma','<b>I don’t know the directions, find no shelter</b>'),('prasīda deva-īśa jagat-nivāsa','<b>be gracious, Lord of gods, home of the world</b>')],
 'Arjuna begs for grace.',
 '<i>Kālānala</i>, the fire that ends the cosmos at pralaya. <i>Prasīda</i>, “be gracious”, is the cry of every devotee in fear. Commentators note: even in terror, Arjuna turns to the same God for refuge.',
 'When frightened by life, turn to the Divine for refuge, not away.',
 '<b>Be gracious</b>, Lord.'),
X(26,'THE KAURAVAS RUSH IN',
 CS('Dhritarashtra’s sons, Bhishma, Drona and Karna rushing toward the mouths','THE WARRIORS'),
 '“All these sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra, together with hosts of kings, and Bhīṣma, Droṇa and that son of a charioteer (Karṇa), along with the chief warriors of our side too,”',
 [('amī ca tvām dhṛtarāṣṭrasya putrāḥ','<b>these sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra into you</b>'),('sarve saha eva avanipāla-saṅghaiḥ','<b>all, with hosts of kings</b>'),('bhīṣmaḥ droṇaḥ sūta-putraḥ tathā asau','<b>Bhīṣma, Droṇa and that charioteer’s son</b>'),('saha asmadīyaiḥ api yodha-mukhyaiḥ','<b>with our chief warriors too</b>')],
 'Arjuna sees the future: the warriors of both sides rushing to death.',
 'This is the “whatever else you wish to see” of 11.7. Commentators note the poignancy: Arjuna sees Bhīṣma and Droṇa, whom he did not want to kill, already entering death. Karṇa is “the charioteer’s son”, as he was known; Arjuna does not yet know Karṇa is his brother. The sentence continues.',
 'The future is already moving. Our part is to do our duty within it.',
 'The warriors <b>rush to their end</b>.'),
X(27,'CRUSHED BETWEEN THE TEETH',
 CS('Warriors caught between fearsome teeth, heads crushed','KĀLA'),
 '“…rush headlong into your fearsome mouths with terrible teeth. Some are seen caught between the teeth, their heads crushed to powder.”',
 [('vaktrāṇi te tvaramāṇāḥ viśanti','<b>they rush into your mouths</b>'),('daṃṣṭrā-karālāni bhayānakāni','<b>terrible with teeth, fearsome</b>'),('kecit vilagnāḥ daśana-antareṣu','<b>some, stuck between the teeth</b>'),('sandṛśyante cūrṇitaiḥ uttama-aṅgaiḥ','<b>are seen with heads crushed</b>')],
 'A terrifying vision of what is to come.',
 'The Gītā does not soften the horror of war. Commentators read it as the truth of time: all bodies, even the greatest heroes’, are consumed. This prepares Arjuna for Kṛṣṇa’s word in 11.32–33.',
 'Death is certain for all bodies. Seeing that clearly can make us wiser about how we live.',
 'All bodies <b>are consumed by time</b>.'),
X(28,'LIKE RIVERS TO THE SEA',
 S('Many rivers rushing into a burning ocean of light','','river','TO THE SEA',NIGHT2),
 '“As the many torrents of rivers rush toward the ocean, so these heroes of the human world enter your blazing mouths.”',
 [('yathā nadīnām bahavaḥ ambu-vegāḥ','<b>as the many water-torrents of rivers</b>'),('samudram eva abhimukhāḥ dravanti','<b>rush toward the ocean</b>'),('tathā tava amī nara-loka-vīrāḥ','<b>so these heroes of the human world</b>'),('viśanti vaktrāṇi abhivijvalanti','<b>enter your blazing mouths</b>')],
 'An image of inevitability: rivers cannot help flowing to the sea.',
 'Compare 2.70, where rivers enter a calm ocean. Here they enter a fire. Commentators: the same image shows peace for the sage and death for the bound. The heroes go willingly, as rivers do, toward their destiny.',
 'Some things flow where they must. Wisdom knows which.',
 'Like rivers <b>rushing to the sea</b>.'),
]
